{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=Northland}}
'''Northland''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The electorate was established for the 1996 election. It was represented by [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] MP [[John Carter (New Zealand politician)|John Carter]] from 1996 to 2011, and then National's [[Mike Sabin]] until his resignation on 30 January 2015. The [[2015 Northland by-election|by-election in March 2015]] was won by [[New Zealand First]] party leader [[Winston Peters]]. Peters was defeated by National's [[Matt King (politician)|Matt King]] in the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]]. King in turn was defeated by the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]'s [[Willow-Jean Prime]] in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]], who became the first Labour MP elected for the area since the party won the predecessor electorate [[Bay of Islands (New Zealand electorate)|Bay of Islands]] in [[1938 New Zealand general election|1938]]. Prime was later defeated in the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 election]] by National's [[Grant McCallum]].

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Northland.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Northland is the northernmost general electorate of New Zealand. The electorate encompasses the entire [[Far North District]] and [[Kaipara District]], and a small rural section of [[Whangarei District]]. At the 2008 election, the town of [[Wellsford]] became part of Northland due to heavy population growth in the [[Rodney (New Zealand electorate)|Rodney]] seat.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=8 |format=PDF |date=14 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123193826/https://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=23 January 2019 |url-status=dead }}</ref> No boundary adjustments were undertaken in the subsequent 2013/14 redistribution.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=8 |format=PDF |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> At the 2019/20 review, the seat lost Wellsford to the new electorate of {{NZ electorate link|Kaipara ki Mahurangi}}, but gained [[Poroti]] and [[Maungakaramea]] from {{NZ electorate link|Whangārei}}. At the 2025 review, the electorate lost the rural communities between [[Whangaruru]] and [[Whananaki]] to {{NZ electorate link|Whangārei}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=17 September 2025 |pages=18–19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>
* [[Taipa-Mangonui|Taipa Bay-Mangonui]] (2,050)
* [[Kaitaia]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Kaitaia|y}})
* [[Kerikeri]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Kerikeri|y}})
* [[Paihia]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Paihia|y}})
* [[Opua]] (860)
* [[Haruru, New Zealand|Haruru Falls]] (870)
* [[Kawakawa, New Zealand|Kawakawa]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Kawakawa|y}})
* [[Moerewa]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Moerewa|y}})
* [[Kaikohe]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Kaikohe|y}})
* [[Dargaville]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Dargaville|y}})
* [[Mangawhai Heads]] (1,086)

==History==
The Northland electorate was created ahead of the introduction of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] voting in the {{NZ election link|1996}}. It was formed from the whole of the [[Far North (New Zealand electorate)|Far North electorate]] (called [[Bay of Islands (New Zealand electorate)|Bay of Islands]] before 1993), and a large section of the [[Hobson (New Zealand electorate)|Hobson seat]].

Former Bay of Islands and then Far North MP [[John Carter (New Zealand politician)|John Carter]] of the National Party was elected MP for Northland in 1996, and was returned at every election until the {{NZ election link|2008}}. Carter left Parliament in June 2011 to take up a post as New Zealand's [[High Commissioner]] to the [[Cook Islands]]. His departure did not result in a by-election, as the vacancy occurred within six months of the next general election.<ref name="Carter valedictory">{{cite news|last=Trevett |first=Claire |title=MP pleads for laughter and leniency as he goes |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10730877 |access-date=25 November 2011 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=8 June 2011}}</ref>

In May 2011 [[Mike Sabin]] was selected as the National Party candidate in place of Carter.<ref>{{cite news| first=Malcolm |last=McMillan |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/4946864/Anti-P-man-Mike-Sabin-takes-safe-National-seat |title=Anti-P man Mike Sabin takes safe National seat |work=stuff.co.nz |date=2 May 2011 |access-date=4 October 2014}}</ref> Sabin had a majority of 11,362 and 9,300 votes in {{NZ election link year|2011}} and {{NZ election link year|2014}}, respectively.<ref name="2011 election" /><ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-35.html |title=Official Count Results – Northland |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=4 October 2014 |date=4 October 2014}}</ref> In December 2014 news media reported that he was under investigation by police over an assault complaint. The reports were not confirmed by the New Zealand Police, the Prime Minister or Sabin himself.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11377730 | title=Cloud over National MP's future | work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] | date=22 December 2014 | first=Adam | last=Bennett | access-date=31 January 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/crime/64362474/nat-mp-in-police-assault-inquiry | title=National MP Mike Sabin in police assault inquiry | work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] | date=21 December 2014 | access-date=31 January 2015 | first=David | last=Lomas}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/northern-advocate/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503450&objectid=11377825 | title=Sabin in the spotlight as assault allegation surfaces | work=[[The Northern Advocate]] | date=22 December 2014 | access-date=31 January 2015 | first=Adam | last=Bennett}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/political/262327/police-tight-lipped-about-assault-allegation | title=Police tight-lipped about assault allegation | work=[[Radio New Zealand]] | date=21 December 2014 | access-date=31 January 2015}}</ref> Sabin resigned from parliament on 30 January 2015 with immediate effect "due to personal issues that were best dealt with outside Parliament."<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA1501/S00163/mike-sabin-announces-resignation-as-northland-mp.htm | title=Mike Sabin announces resignation as Northland MP | work=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] | date=30 January 2015 | access-date=30 January 2015}}</ref> The resignation forced a [[2015 Northland by-election|by-election in the electorate]].<ref name="NZH resignation">{{cite news | title=John Key says National did not ask Mike Sabin to quit after MP resigns 'due to personal issues' | url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11394142 | access-date=31 January 2015 | work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] | date=30 January 2015}}</ref>

The by-election in March 2015 was won by [[New Zealand First]] party leader [[Winston Peters]]. Peters was later defeated by National's [[Matt King (politician)|Matt King]] in the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]].<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/northern-advocate/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503450&objectid=11925892|title=King beats kingmaker in nail-biting race for Northland seat|date=24 September 2017 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|access-date=24 September 2017}}</ref>

The electorate is one where National traditionally performs well; Labour did not win a general electorate north of Wellsford in any election from [[1943 New Zealand general election|1943]] to [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017]] besides a one-off victory in [[Whangarei (New Zealand electorate)|Whangarei]] in [[1972 New Zealand general election|1972]]. However, in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]], Northland elected its first ever Labour MP and the first Labour MP for the area for 77 years.

The upper North Island is also a place where New Zealand First has one of its strongest voter bases; in 1996, [[Ian Peters]] and [[Frank Grover]] of the Alliance beat Labour's candidate into fourth place and nine percent of the vote. Third parties do well in Northland – at the [[1960 New Zealand general election|1960]] and [[1963 New Zealand general election|1963]] elections, [[Social Credit Party (New Zealand)|Social Credit]] candidate [[Vernon Cracknell]] came runner up in Hobson, before taking the seat with 48 percent of the vote in [[1966 New Zealand general election|1966]]. The area had previously been receptive to social credit theory – [[Harold Rushworth]] of the credit-influenced [[Country Party (New Zealand)|Country Party]] had held Bay of Islands for three terms, from [[1928 New Zealand general election|1928]] to [[1938 New Zealand general election|1938]].

